    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Neurology consultation in two weeks with nerve conduction studies to confirm or exclude a late post-frostbite neuropathy, entrapment, or another polyneuropathy, and to advise on treatment that does not cause drowsiness. Guideline: Neuropathic pain in adults: pharmacological management in non-specialist settings (CG173) (2013, updated 2020); Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024) [S]</item><item>Cold protection and hand care: liner gloves under mittens, heated gloves and hand warmers outdoors, warming the hands before fine work, no cold-water hand washing, and checking the fingers for skin damage. Guideline: Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Frostbite: 2024 Update (McIntosh SE, et al. Wilderness Environ Med 2024;35(2):183-197) (2024) [S]</item><item>Blood tests for other causes of neuropathy: glucose and HbA1c, vitamin B12, TSH, kidney function, liver enzyme and hemoglobin; PHQ-9 for mood with chronic pain. Guideline: Neuropathic pain in adults: pharmacological management in non-specialist settings (CG173) (2013, updated 2020); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023) [S]</item></list></text>

